# Every key here has a working default. The app boots, seeds, and completes the
# full booking -> confirm -> assign -> outreach flow with NO .env at all.
#
# Copy to .env only when you are switching something on. Anything left unset
# reports itself as not-configured in /api/health and in the UI banner --
# nothing is ever simulated to cover a gap.

# --- Agent -------------------------------------------------------------------
# stub: rail-driven state machine calling the real tools. Answers only what
#       someone anticipated -- every reply is assembled from tool results.
# live: a real model reads the conversation and writes its own words. This is
#       what makes it unscripted. Same eight tools, same guards either way.
LLM_MODE=stub

# --- The one key you need for live replies ---
# Put your OpenAI key here and set LLM_MODE=live above. Nothing else is needed.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# gpt-5.6-luna is the cheapest tier of the 5.6 family and does function
# calling. It is a *reasoning* model, which is why the code speaks the
# Responses API rather than chat completions -- see app/agent/providers.py.
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5.6-luna
# Reasoning tokens are spent from the same budget as the reply, so too small a
# ceiling returns an empty message rather than a short one.
# OPENAI_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=4096
# minimal | low | medium | high. A buyer is watching a spinner, and picking a
# car out of five search results is not a hard reasoning problem.
# OPENAI_REASONING_EFFORT=low
# Only for Azure or an OpenAI-compatible gateway.
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=

# Anthropic is supported too; set LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic to use it.
# LLM_PROVIDER=openai
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-5

# --- Public demo -------------------------------------------------------------
# Let anybody with the URL in, as a sales rep, with no password. There is a
# "Log in as a sales manager" button in the header for the other half.
#
# This is the one setting here that hands a stranger a dealership's buyer list.
# Everything a rep can see they can see: names, phone numbers, email addresses,
# call transcripts and call recordings, which are somebody's actual voice. Only
# ever point it at demo data -- `make reset-db && make seed-demo` builds a
# database where every address is @example.invalid and cannot be reached.
#
# Managers still need a password, so the team page, the assistant's
# instructions and publishing stay shut.
# PUBLIC_DEMO=true
# PUBLIC_DEMO_EMAIL=marcus.vale@example.invalid   # defaults to the first active rep

# --- Email -------------------------------------------------------------------
# outbox: records a real outreach row and mirrors it into the buyer's chat
#         thread. Sends nothing, and says so on every surface.
# gmail:  real delivery. Use a Google Workspace service account with
#         domain-wide delegation -- a personal @gmail.com refresh token expires
#         after 7 days in External/Testing, quite possibly the morning of a demo.
# resend: real delivery over the REST API. This is what the deployment is
#         meant to use; the domain has to be verified in Resend first.
EMAIL_SENDER=outbox
# GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON=/path/to/service-account.json
# GMAIL_IMPERSONATE=sales@yourdealership.com
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...

# --- Email replies -----------------------------------------------------------
# SENDING_DOMAIN does three jobs and each breaks differently if it is wrong:
# outbound builds `Reply-To: reply+<token>@here`; the Cloudflare catch-all on
# the same domain is what routes those replies back to /api/inbound-email; and
# it decides who may put their own address in a From.
#
# That last one is what lets founder@ and cto@ each answer under their own
# name. The provider verifies the *domain*, not the mailbox, so verifying
# linerai.us once makes every address on it legal to send as -- a third person
# is a row in the users table and no new credential. An account whose address
# is NOT on this domain falls back to SENDING_FROM and the composer says so,
# because a From a provider has not verified gets the whole message rejected.
#
# SENDING_FROM is the DEALERSHIP's address, and an address rather than a
# header. Liner's own mail is not configured here at all -- it goes from
# SUPPORT_EMAIL, which `is_ours` already reads.
#
# Two mailboxes on purpose. A buyer looking at a booking confirmation may
# compose a *fresh* message back rather than pressing Reply, and anything
# addressed to support@ is routed to /ops -- so with one shared mailbox that
# buyer reached Liner instead of the dealership, silently. sales@ is also
# simply what a car dealership's mail comes from.
#
# The display name on an envelope is served, never configured: mail from the
# dealership is signed with the dealership's own name out of the database, and
# mail from /ops is signed "Liner". A name written here would be a second copy
# of a fact that already exists, and this line is the one people copy -- it
# used to read `Riverside Auto <support@linerai.us>`, so every deployment
# started from this file mailed its buyers as a fixture car dealership. A name
# left here now is dropped rather than sent.
# SENDING_DOMAIN=linerai.us
# SENDING_FROM=sales@linerai.us
#
# WEBHOOK_SECRET is shared with the Cloudflare Worker and is the only thing in
# front of an endpoint that writes into a buyer's history -- no session guards
# it, because Cloudflare has none to send. It defaults to a development value
# so the inbound path can be tested; ENV=production refuses to boot on it.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Set the identical value on the Worker: wrangler secret put WEBHOOK_SECRET
# Setup: backend/app/integrations/email/worker/README.md
# WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Liner answering email ---------------------------------------------------
# OFF, and off by default is the point: taking over a mailbox is a decision a
# dealership makes, the same shape as VOICE_PROVIDER. There are two switches
# and the stricter always wins -- this one is the deployment's answer and needs
# a restart; the other is a toggle on /app/email that takes effect on the next
# delivery, because a kill switch is reached for while something is going wrong
# and cannot wait for a deploy.
# EMAIL_AGENT=true

# HOW LONG LINER WAITS BEFORE ANSWERING. Every reply waits, including the
# first: a dealership that answers three seconds after a buyer wrote is
# obviously a robot, and the wait buys something besides -- a window in which a
# rep reads the message and takes the thread over before anything goes out. A
# rep answering inside it cancels the queued reply, so the buyer gets one email
# and not two.
#
# A gap *between* replies falls out of the same number rather than being a
# second rule. A tuning value, so it lives here rather than in the dashboard --
# unlike the kill switch, which is reached for while something is going wrong.
# EMAIL_REPLY_COOLDOWN_MINUTES=60

# And a ceiling across every correspondent per hour. The per-correspondent
# clock stops one loop; a spam run across five hundred addresses walks past it,
# because every one is a first contact. Breaching this throws the kill switch
# rather than waiting for somebody to wake up.
# EMAIL_REPLIES_PER_HOUR=30

# --- Voice -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Empty means voice is off, and /call says so, even with an OpenAI key present.
# Taking calls is a decision a dealership makes, not a side effect of setting
# up the chat agent. `openai` is the only implementation.
# VOICE_PROVIDER=openai
# Optional. Empty reuses OPENAI_API_KEY, which is the normal case -- set this
# only to bill voice to a different project.
# VOICE_PROVIDER_KEY=

# THE COST LEVER. gpt-realtime-mini is roughly a third of the flagship
# ($10/$20 per million audio tokens against $32/$64), and for qualifying a
# buyer and booking a slot it is very likely enough. Change this one line and
# restart; the cost report re-prices itself, because the rates follow the
# model rather than being pinned separately.
# VOICE_MODEL=gpt-realtime-mini

# marin | coral | shimmer | sage | alloy | ash | ballad | echo | verse | cedar
# VOICE_VOICE=marin

# What the caller hears on connect, before the microphone opens. Played by the
# browser, not spoken by the model -- so it is the same words every time, and
# costs no output audio tokens. Empty plays a short tone; point it at a
# recording of the greeting text on the Liner setup page.
# VOICE_GREETING_AUDIO=https://your-cdn/greeting.mp3

# The other two brakes on a call that re-bills its whole history every turn.
# VOICE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=1200
# VOICE_RETENTION_RATIO=0.8

# Transcribing the buyer is billed SEPARATELY from the call and buys a
# readable record, nothing more -- the model hears the audio directly and
# never reads the text. The one thing here you can switch off without
# changing what the assistant can do.
# VOICE_TRANSCRIBE=false

# Transcribe the buyer's own microphone track again once the call has ended,
# and keep that version. The live transcriber works on a stream with no future
# context and no second pass, which is why "E-Class" came back as 比克拉斯;
# this one gets the whole call as context and can be retried. A separate charge
# again, and worth having as well as the live pass rather than instead of it --
# the live one is what puts words on the rail while a rep is watching.
# VOICE_TRANSCRIBE_AFTER=false
# VOICE_TRANSCRIBE_AFTER_MODEL=gpt-4o-transcribe

# Only if a published rate moves before this repository catches up. Unset, the
# rates follow VOICE_MODEL; set one and it wins for every model.
# VOICE_PRICE_AUDIO_IN=32
# VOICE_PRICE_AUDIO_OUT=64
# VOICE_PRICE_CACHED_IN=0.4

# --- Which dealership this instance is ---------------------------------------
# Picks backend/config/dealerships/<name>.yaml. Unset uses
# backend/config/dealership.yaml, which is the Riverside Auto fixture.
#
# It belongs here rather than only on the seed command: the running server
# reads it too -- it is how /api/showroom knows whose colour and logo to serve,
# and which folder under backend/var/inventory/ a crawl writes into.
#
# Switching is a reseed, not a second tenant: no table carries a dealership id
# and the app holds exactly one at a time. A profile only seeds what it
# actually states -- Riverside's fourteen fixture cars and its written policy
# answers do not travel to a real prospect, because a made-up doc fee quoted
# to a real buyer is worse than no answer. Runbook: docs/DEMO.md
# DEALERSHIP=craigandlandreth

# --- Inventory ingest --------------------------------------------------------
# Unset means the import screen offers CSV only, which needs no configuration.
# For the local fixture site: make fixture-site, then set this to
# http://127.0.0.1:8100
# SCRAPER_BASE_URL=
# SCRAPER_USER_AGENT=LinerAI-Ingest/0.1 (+https://liner.ai/bot)
# SCRAPER_RATE_LIMIT=1.5

# Which lot to keep when a listing page carries several. A Dealer Car Search
# site can show three stores' stock in one list, each card carrying its own
# dealer id -- and this app holds exactly one dealership, so without this
# Liner offers a buyer a car two hours from the showroom it says it is in.
# SCRAPER_DEALER_ID=1123
#
# Download each car's listing photo into backend/var/inventory/<dealer>/
# instead of hotlinking the dealer's image host. One request per car, so it is
# off by default; on for a demo, where their CDN 404ing a sold car mid-
# presentation is a failure on the screen somebody is watching. One photo per
# car -- the rest live on each detail page and are not worth 481 fetches.
# SCRAPER_SAVE_PHOTOS=true

# --- Signing in ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Wrong passwords one account may take in a window before the form starts
# refusing with a 429. Keyed on the *account*, never the caller's address:
# behind nginx or Cloudflare every request carries the proxy's IP unless
# --proxy-headers is set, so an IP key would let one bot lock out everybody at
# once. A correct password clears the count.
#
# In-process, like the event bus -- which is why this system already runs a
# single uvicorn worker. Raise the window rather than the count to slow a
# guess down further; eight tries is about what a person who genuinely forgot
# theirs needs.
# LOGIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS=8
# LOGIN_WINDOW_SECONDS=300

# --- Core --------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///./backend/liner.db
# ENV=development
# SESSION_SECRET=   # startup REFUSES to run with the dev default if ENV=production
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# Seeded logins, one per role: the manager sees every lead, the team page and
# the assistant settings; reps work the floor. Both default to 'liner-dev',
# which is published in this repo -- so with ENV=production, startup refuses to
# run until each is set to something real AND the two differ. Changing them
# takes effect on the next `make reset-db`, which wipes the database.
# MANAGER_PASSWORD=
# REP_PASSWORD=

# Us, not the dealership: founder@linerai.us and cto@linerai.us. They live in
# their own table (`ops_users`), sign in at /login?as=owner and land on /ops --
# the demos people book with us and the mail they send. A dealership's staff
# cannot reach any of it, and an ops session is refused by every one of their
# endpoints in return.
#
# One key per person, because a shared password is not a login: with two people
# on one value a leak cannot be traced to anybody, and revoking it locks out
# whoever did not leak it. Generate them separately:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 24
#
# Same production rule as the two above -- the dev default is published in this
# repository, so ENV=production refuses to boot until each is real and no two
# of the four match. They are read at seed time, so after changing one on a
# live box use `make set-password EMAIL=founder@linerai.us` rather than
# expecting a restart to pick it up.
# FOUNDER_PASSWORD=
# CTO_PASSWORD=
#
# The old shared key. Still read as the fallback for either account above, so
# an .env written before the split keeps working -- set the two and drop this.
# OWNER_PASSWORD=

# --- Who outbound email may reach --------------------------------------------
# One setting, and its name is the rule. Three ways to write it:
#
#   OUTBOUND_ONLY_TO=                      nobody -- every send is refused
#   OUTBOUND_ONLY_TO=me@x.com,you@y.com    only those addresses
#   OUTBOUND_ONLY_TO=everyone              no restriction at all
#
# It gates SENDING only. Nothing has ever filtered incoming mail: anyone can
# write to your domain and it lands, and a reply that cannot be placed is kept
# rather than dropped.
#
# 'everyone' is a word rather than an empty value on purpose -- empty meaning
# "mail anybody" is the kind of default that goes wrong in silence.
OUTBOUND_ONLY_TO=
